<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 THE PROBLEM -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Fixed-Depth Trap</div>
<h2>Every Step Gets the Same Treatment</h2>
<p>
  World models predict how environments evolve: given a state and an action, what happens next?
  This is the core loop of model-based reinforcement learning — the agent imagines thousands
  of future trajectories inside a learned simulator, plans the best actions, then acts.
</p>
<p>
  The problem is that not all transitions are equally hard. When an agent navigates an empty
  corridor ("go north"), the world state barely changes — a single linear transformation would
  suffice. But when a catalyst is added to a beaker, triggering a multi-step chemical reaction,
  the dynamics are far more complex, requiring deep iterative reasoning to predict correctly.
</p>
<p>
  Today's world models are <em>fixed-depth</em>: a stack of L unique transformer layers, always
  applied in full, for every single transition. The same 100-layer compute budget goes to
  "go north" as to "ignite the reaction." This creates two failure modes simultaneously.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">fixed-depth vs adaptive compute — same model, different transitions</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-problem" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The left panel shows a fixed-depth model applying all 8 layers uniformly to every transition —
  a trivial navigation step gets the same budget as a complex multi-object interaction.
  The wasted compute on simple steps adds up over thousands of rollout iterations.
  Worse, this uniformity means the model must compromise: it can't afford the 12 layers that
  the truly hard transitions need, so it allocates a middle-ground depth that undershoots
  on complexity and overshoots on simplicity.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Compounding errors:</strong> small prediction mistakes at each step accumulate
    exponentially over long horizons. Deeper = better per-step quality, but 10× more parameters
    and 10× more inference cost for 1000 rollout steps.</li>
  <li><strong>Deployment pressure:</strong> long-horizon planning requires running the dynamics
    model hundreds of times in sequence. A model that is too deep to be real-time is useless
    for embodied AI and game environments.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  LoopWM resolves both with a single insight: <strong>make depth a function of transition
  complexity, not a fixed hyperparameter</strong>.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 LOOPED ARCHITECTURES -->
<section id="s-loop" data-code="loop">
<div class="section-no">02 — Looped Architectures</div>
<h2>One Block, Applied Iteratively</h2>
<p>
  A standard transformer with L layers has L separate sets of parameters — each layer has
  its own weights, independent of every other layer. Depth and parameter count are tightly coupled.
  Double the depth, double the parameters.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Looped (universal) transformers</strong> break this coupling by sharing parameters across
  depth. Instead of L unique blocks, there is one block <em>R</em> applied T times in sequence.
  The same weights are reused at every iteration; only the hidden state changes. Effective depth
  is T × (layers in R), but the parameter count is just 1 × (layers in R).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">parameter count: fixed-depth (L unique layers) vs looped (1 shared block × T)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-loop" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  This is not just a memory trick. Theoretical work shows looped transformers can simulate arbitrary
  iterative algorithms — gradient descent, Newton's method, dynamic programming — with constant
  parameter count. Empirically, a looped LM with 1 shared block achieves 2–3× parameter efficiency
  over an equivalent fixed-depth model on language tasks.
</p>
<p>
  LoopWM is the <em>first application of this idea to world modelling</em>. And it turns out that
  world models have a structural property that makes looped architectures especially natural: physical
  dynamics are themselves an iterative process. The same governing law (Newton's equations,
  chemical kinetics) applies repeatedly across time. The looped transformer's computation graph —
  a shared update operator applied repeatedly to a hidden state — is directly isomorphic to this structure.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Looped update rule (Eq. 5 from paper)</div>
  <span class="math-var">h</span><sup>(t+1)</sup> = <span class="math-var">Ā</span> <span class="math-var">h</span><sup>(t)</sup> + <span class="math-var">B̄ e</span> + <span class="math-var">R̄</span>(<span class="math-var">h</span><sup>(t)</sup>, <span class="math-var">e</span>)
  <span class="math-comment">   ← same weights Ā, B̄, R̄ at every iteration t</span>
</div>
<p>
  Here <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">e</span> is the conditioning
  signal (observation + action, processed once by the prelude), <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">Ā</span>
  governs state retention, <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">B̄</span>
  injects the input, and <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">R̄</span>
  captures the transformer nonlinearities (attention + FFN).
  The same three matrices apply at t=0, t=1, …, t=T−1.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ARCHITECTURE -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — LoopWM Architecture</div>
<h2>Prelude → Loop → Coda, with Spectral Stability</h2>
<p>
  LoopWM follows a prelude–recurrent–coda (PRC) design. The observation and action are
  processed once by the prelude; the looped block refines the hidden state T times; the coda
  projects the final hidden state to prediction heads for reward, continuation, and next-observation.
</p>

