<h3>The Spectral Stability Constraint</h3>
<p>
  Looped architectures have a well-known training instability: if the hidden state isn't
  carefully controlled, it can grow or shrink exponentially as T increases. Over a long rollout
  of 500 environment steps, this would cause catastrophic divergence.
</p>
<p>
  LoopWM solves this by construction. The state-retention matrix <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">Ā</span>
  is parameterized as the exponential of a strictly negative diagonal matrix:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <span class="math-comment">Continuous-time diagonal: </span><span class="math-var">A</span> = diag(−exp(<span class="math-var">a</span>)), &nbsp; <span class="math-var">a</span> ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup> (learnable)<br/>
  <span class="math-comment">Zero-order hold:           </span><span class="math-var">Ā</span> = exp(Δ · <span class="math-var">A</span>), &nbsp; Δ ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup><sub>&gt;0</sub> (learnable)<br/>
  <span class="math-comment">Result:                    </span>all eigenvalues of <span class="math-var">Ā</span> ∈ (0, 1) &nbsp; <em>by construction</em>
</div>
<p>
  Since every entry of Δ·A is strictly negative, the matrix exponential maps them into (0,1).
  This means <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">ρ(Ā) &lt; 1</span>
  regardless of how many loop iterations are taken. No gradient clipping, no spectral normalization
  penalties, no sensitive tuning required — the constraint is baked into the parameterization.
</p>
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">hidden state norm ‖h‖ over 50 rollout steps — constrained vs unconstrained</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-stability" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>
<p>
  Without the spectral constraint, the latent state norm can grow exponentially across rollout
  steps (red line), causing numerical instability and garbage predictions. With ρ(Ā) &lt; 1,
  the dynamics remain bounded no matter how many steps are unrolled — a formal guarantee absent
  from standard autoregressive world models.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ADAPTIVE COMPUTATION -->
<section id="s-adaptive" data-code="adaptive">
<div class="section-no">04 — Adaptive Computation</div>
<h2>How Many Loops Does This Transition Need?</h2>
<p>
  With a looped architecture in place, a natural question arises: does every transition need
  the same number of inner-loop iterations? The answer is emphatically no. And making T
  per-transition rather than global is where LoopWM achieves its most compelling efficiency.
</p>
<p>
  A lightweight <strong>exit gate</strong> — a single linear layer followed by sigmoid — is trained
  alongside the dynamics core. At each iteration t, the gate evaluates the current hidden state
  and outputs an exit probability g<sup>(t)</sup>. If g<sup>(t)</sup> exceeds threshold τ, the loop terminates early.
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <span class="math-comment">Exit gate: </span>g<sup>(t)</sup> = σ(w<sub>g</sub><sup>⊤</sup> h<sup>(t)</sup> + b<sub>g</sub>)
  <span class="math-comment">   ← converged if g<sup>(t)</sup> &gt; τ</span>
</div>
<p>
  This maps directly onto the non-uniform computational demands of environment simulation.
  A simple navigation action ("go east") produces a nearly linear state change — the hidden
  state converges after 1–2 iterations. A complex action ("add catalyst to beaker, observe
  reaction") requires iterative latent refinement over 6–8 iterations.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">inner-loop iterations T per transition step — click to switch sequence type</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interact</span>
  </div>
