<p>
  The easy rollout shows mostly T=1–2 iterations (green bars): simple navigation, looking around,
  picking up a single object. The compute overhead is minimal. The complex rollout shows the
  same 10-step sequence but with multi-step chemistry interactions — the exit gate runs 6–8
  iterations on the hard steps, automatically allocating depth where it matters.
</p>
<p>
  The paper notes that in the most favourable cases — long rollouts dominated by simple
  transitions interspersed with rare complex events — the aggregate FLOP reduction relative
  to a fixed-depth model can reach <strong>up to two orders of magnitude</strong>.
  This makes LoopWM uniquely suited for long-horizon planning, where the dynamics model
  runs hundreds or thousands of times per decision.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Entropy regularization:</strong> without a training signal encouraging the gate to explore different exit points, it collapses to always-exit-at-t=1 (minimum compute) or never-exit (maximum compute). LoopWM adds an entropy penalty on the gate's exit distribution to keep it exploratory:
  L<sub>ent</sub> = −α · E[Σ<sub>t</sub> H(g<sup>(t)</sup>)], where H is binary entropy.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 DEFERRED DECODING -->
<section id="s-deferred" data-code="deferred">
<div class="section-no">05 — Deferred Decoding</div>
<h2>Five Actions, One Decode</h2>
<p>
  Standard world models decode — reconstruct the observation, predict the reward, and emit
  the continuation flag — at every single rollout step. Over a K-step planning horizon, that
  means K full decoder invocations, each consuming significant FLOPs and forcing the latent
  state to maintain a reconstruction-ready representation at every intermediate step.
</p>
<p>
  LoopWM introduces <strong>deferred decoding (DD)</strong>: run all K action-conditioned latent
  transitions silently, then decode exactly once at the terminal step. This isn't just an
  efficiency trick — it changes what the latent trajectory learns to encode. Without per-step
  reconstruction pressure, the hidden states can capture temporally extended, action-relevant
  structure rather than per-step visual detail.
</p>
<p>
  This mirrors the "encode → think → decode" paradigm from language reasoning, applied here
  to environment simulation: the model ingests an initial observation, rolls out K latent
  transitions driven by the action sequence, and only surfaces to observation space at the end.
</p>

<!-- Deferred decoding viz -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">per-step decode vs deferred decode — K=5 action horizon</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-deferred" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  In the top row (per-step), each of the 5 transitions triggers a full decoder call (red boxes).
  In the bottom row (deferred), transitions 1–4 run purely in latent space; only step 5 decodes
  — saving 4 full decoder forward passes per plan evaluation. When combined with the adaptive
  exit gate (§04), the total FLOP reduction can reach two orders of magnitude for long planning
  horizons with simple dynamics.
</p>
<h3>Training the Deferred Decoder</h3>
<p>
  Training with K-step latent rollouts is unstable at large K: gradients must backpropagate
  through K × T shared-parameter applications. LoopWM uses a progressive horizon curriculum:
  begin with K=1 (standard per-step), increase K gradually until K reaches K<sub>max</sub>.
  This lets the dynamics first learn accurate single-step transitions before being challenged
  with longer decode-free horizons.
</p>
<p>
  A latent consistency loss provides soft intermediate supervision without requiring a full
  decoder: encode each ground-truth intermediate observation into a reference embedding, then
  align the predicted hidden state to it via a lightweight projection head with stop-gradient.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 RESULTS -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>A 1B Model That Beats Frontier LLMs at World Modelling</h2>
<p>
  LoopWM is evaluated on two text-based environment simulation benchmarks: <strong>ScienceWorld</strong>
  (25 science-lab task categories including boiling, freezing, genetics, chemical reactions)
  and <strong>AlfWorld</strong> (household object manipulation: clean, heat, cool, look, pick).
  The evaluation protocol: feed the model 5 consecutive actions; it predicts the resulting world state.
</p>
<p>
  Results are compared against frontier LLMs used as world simulators zero-shot — Claude Opus 4.6 Max,
  Gemini 3 Flash Preview Thinking, and Qwen 3.5 Flash — all vastly larger than LoopWM's ~1B parameters.
</p>

